1294
00:44:37,124 --> 00:44:39,264
The rooftop woman is talking.

1295
00:44:39,264 --> 00:44:40,924
- We don't listen to each other. - We don't listen to each other.

1296
00:44:40,924 --> 00:44:42,934
- We don't listen to each other. - It doesn't matter.

1297
00:44:43,194 --> 00:44:45,704
I'll only know for sure when I go to the third place,

1298
00:44:46,034 --> 00:44:47,664
but today is the most difficult day out of all the days I came.

1299
00:44:48,104 --> 00:44:50,574
Do you remember where we went on your first appearance?

1300
00:44:51,074 --> 00:44:53,504
(Error)

1301
00:44:53,574 --> 00:44:54,604
I don't.

1302
00:44:54,844 --> 00:44:55,874
What did he say?

1303
00:44:55,874 --> 00:44:58,044
- No, I don't. - No, I don't.

1304
00:44:58,614 --> 00:45:00,584
- Let us decide. - This is hard.

1305
00:45:00,584 --> 00:45:02,814
- Good. Thank you. - Thank you.

1306
00:45:02,814 --> 00:45:04,154
(Beef bone soup course meal, 1. It doesn't taste like one.)

1307
00:45:04,154 --> 00:45:09,024
Kyeong Hwan and Woo Jae joined us as the guests.

1308
00:45:09,554 --> 00:45:11,594
We should ask them something.

1309
00:45:11,594 --> 00:45:12,824
For the first time,

1310
00:45:12,824 --> 00:45:15,764
the production crew requested that we ask the guests some questions.

1311
00:45:16,164 --> 00:45:18,134
We made the request.

1312
00:45:18,864 --> 00:45:21,664
- You requested it? - Yes, I requested it to the writer.

1313
00:45:21,764 --> 00:45:24,734
Woo Jae is a model.

1314
00:45:24,734 --> 00:45:25,844
That's right.

1315
00:45:26,074 --> 00:45:27,644
- Seriously... - Right, you're a model.

1316
00:45:27,644 --> 00:45:29,274
I can feel how unwilling he is.

1317
00:45:29,274 --> 00:45:30,314
But...

1318
00:45:30,874 --> 00:45:33,984
Right, I heard that. Woo Jae, I really have a question.

1319
00:45:33,984 --> 00:45:35,114
He's releasing an album.

1320
00:45:35,344 --> 00:45:36,684
Yes, he's releasing a song.

1321
00:45:36,684 --> 00:45:38,784
It's a secret project.

1322
00:45:38,784 --> 00:45:40,554
I said it in front of six cameras. Will it be okay?

1323
00:45:40,554 --> 00:45:42,494
Yes. I'll promote it in secret too.

1324
00:45:42,754 --> 00:45:44,254
Will you be using a pseudonym?

1325
00:45:44,254 --> 00:45:45,864
Use a pseudonym. "Pork Backbone Soup."

1326
00:45:45,994 --> 00:45:48,264
(Pork Backbone Soup, singer, previously Joo Woo Jae)

1327
00:45:48,264 --> 00:45:50,134
You're releasing an album, not a digital single?

1328
00:45:50,134 --> 00:45:51,594
- Yes. - My gosh.

1329
00:45:51,594 --> 00:45:53,504
- An album... - How many songs?

1330
00:45:53,504 --> 00:45:54,504
Five.

1331
00:45:54,504 --> 00:45:55,674
- Really? - Really?

1332
00:45:55,734 --> 00:45:57,734
Are we supposed to guess if this is real or fake?

1333
00:45:57,734 --> 00:45:58,774
It's Sixth Sense.

1334
00:45:58,774 --> 00:46:00,804
(Sixth Sense crew, is this album real?)

1335
00:46:00,804 --> 00:46:04,414
Kyeong Hwan has been appearing on more shows.

1336
00:46:04,414 --> 00:46:06,844
- Kyeong Hwan. - But...

1337
00:46:06,844 --> 00:46:09,284
you're asking questions as if you're doing homework.

1338
00:46:09,284 --> 00:46:11,514
We had an order from the production crew.

1339
00:46:12,084 --> 00:46:15,154
Kyeong Hwan's become busy recently.

1340
00:46:15,154 --> 00:46:17,794
- I'm very busy. - How are you doing?

1341
00:46:18,354 --> 00:46:20,594
They say every dog has its day,

1342
00:46:20,594 --> 00:46:23,364
but I don't want the day to end.

1343
00:46:24,064 --> 00:46:25,604
Did you just jab at me?

1344
00:46:26,934 --> 00:46:29,534
Someone I know has been having his day for so long,

1345
00:46:29,534 --> 00:46:31,204
and I aspire to be like him.

1346
00:46:31,204 --> 00:46:32,574
It doesn't end in one day.

1347
00:46:32,574 --> 00:46:34,444
(I see.)

1348
00:46:34,874 --> 00:46:37,274
Our answers aren't that interesting compared to the questions.

1349
00:46:37,274 --> 00:46:39,114
I think it's better if you don't ask us anything.

1350
00:46:39,114 --> 00:46:41,014
(Taking back their request to ask them questions)

1351
00:46:41,014 --> 00:46:43,614
Anyway, based on the restaurants,

1352
00:46:43,614 --> 00:46:45,054
the second one looks overwhelmingly more real...

1353
00:46:45,054 --> 00:46:46,484
compared to the first one.

1354
00:46:46,484 --> 00:46:47,824
- It overwhelmingly is. - That's right.

1355
00:46:47,824 --> 00:46:50,054
- It looked so real. - Just based on the restaurants,

1356
00:46:50,154 --> 00:46:51,594
the second place looks real.

1357
00:46:51,594 --> 00:46:54,294
The chef was young too.

1358
00:46:54,294 --> 00:46:55,694
- He was born in 1994. - Yes, born in 1994.

1359
00:46:55,694 --> 00:46:59,104
Nowadays, a lot of young people...

1360
00:46:59,104 --> 00:47:01,164
make fusion dishes.

1361
00:47:01,164 --> 00:47:02,604
That goes without saying.

1362
00:47:03,004 --> 00:47:05,034
So him cooking beef bone soup...

1363
00:47:05,434 --> 00:47:06,444
Hold on.

1364
00:47:06,904 --> 00:47:08,244
I think the conversation is stuck.

1365
00:47:09,144 --> 00:47:11,614
Why can't you complete your sentences today?

1366
00:47:11,644 --> 00:47:14,444
For him to complete the sentence,

1367
00:47:14,444 --> 00:47:16,484
he needs responses like, "That's right."

1368
00:47:16,484 --> 00:47:17,914
That goes without saying.

1369
00:47:17,914 --> 00:47:19,784
But you responded with, "That goes without saying."

1370
00:47:19,784 --> 00:47:21,124
So he can't complete his sentence.

1371
00:47:21,124 --> 00:47:22,254
I thought we were playing "Of Course" game.

1372
00:47:22,924 --> 00:47:24,454
I can tell...

1373
00:47:24,454 --> 00:47:25,964
- you guys are really close. - Right.

1374
00:47:26,064 --> 00:47:28,394
We see each other at least once or twice a week.

1375
00:47:28,394 --> 00:47:29,464
That's right.

1376
00:47:29,464 --> 00:47:30,994
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1377
00:47:31,194 --> 00:47:32,504
Thank you.

1378
00:47:32,504 --> 00:47:33,634
What will be here?

1379
00:47:34,234 --> 00:47:35,604
Exactly. What can be here?

1380
00:47:35,604 --> 00:47:37,134
- It's a shopping complex. - What's here?

1381
00:47:37,134 --> 00:47:38,134
Livestock...

1382
00:47:38,604 --> 00:47:41,674
(What kind of Korean food will it be among the many cram schools?)

1383
00:47:41,944 --> 00:47:44,174
Once we go to this restaurant, we'll know the answer.

1384
00:47:46,444 --> 00:47:47,544
Here?

1385
00:47:47,544 --> 00:47:48,644
(Is this the place?)

1386
00:47:48,644 --> 00:47:50,554
- Hold on. - It's way too fake.

1387
00:47:50,554 --> 00:47:51,614
It looks very fake.

1388
00:47:51,614 --> 00:47:53,154
(A building with many cram schools)

1389
00:47:53,154 --> 00:47:58,194
(Behind it is a restaurant.)

1390
00:47:58,294 --> 00:47:59,724
It looks like it was...

1391
00:47:59,724 --> 00:48:01,094
- hurriedly painted recently. - It looks newly renovated.

1392
00:48:01,094 --> 00:48:03,134
It makes me stop in my tracks.

1393
00:48:03,234 --> 00:48:04,964
It's so obviously fake.

1394
00:48:05,464 --> 00:48:07,764
It's way too new. Look at the paint.

1395
00:48:07,764 --> 00:48:10,034
- This... - They painted this yesterday.

1396
00:48:10,834 --> 00:48:12,744
That could be true.

1397
00:48:13,244 --> 00:48:14,974
What food is it?

1398
00:48:14,974 --> 00:48:16,444
(What Korean dish is hidden here?)

1399
00:48:16,814 --> 00:48:19,284
(They enter cautiously.)

1400
00:48:20,544 --> 00:48:23,084
(What is this restaurant?)

1401
00:48:23,154 --> 00:48:24,384
Hello.

1402
00:48:24,984 --> 00:48:28,224
- All the restaurants today... - They're similar.

1403
00:48:28,224 --> 00:48:29,654
They sell wine too?

1404
00:48:30,254 --> 00:48:31,424
It looks fake again.

1405
00:48:31,424 --> 00:48:32,924
(A wine cellar and black tables?)

1406
00:48:33,394 --> 00:48:35,734
(Observing carefully)

1407
00:48:36,664 --> 00:48:37,664
This...

1408
00:48:39,164 --> 00:48:42,164
(What is it?)

1409
00:48:42,462 --> 00:48:44,962
(Observing carefully)

1410
00:48:45,832 --> 00:48:46,862
This...

1411
00:48:48,332 --> 00:48:50,702
(What is it?)

1412
00:48:51,362 --> 00:48:53,572
"Sea gimbap, fruit gimbap?" This...

1413
00:48:54,272 --> 00:48:55,432
(Green gimbap, cabbage tuna gimbap, fruit gimbap, sea gimbap...)

1414
00:48:55,432 --> 00:49:00,442
(This place is a gimbap restaurant?)

1415
00:49:00,442 --> 00:49:02,912
"Bingsu gimbap?" This is madness.

1416
00:49:03,912 --> 00:49:04,912
(Among various gimbap menus...)

1417
00:49:04,912 --> 00:49:07,482
It doesn't make sense. I've never seen bingsu gimbap.

1418
00:49:07,682 --> 00:49:09,182
Why do they sell wine in a gimbap restaurant?

1419
00:49:09,422 --> 00:49:11,582
It looks like it's for decoration.

1420
00:49:11,982 --> 00:49:13,952
This looks way too fake.

1421
00:49:14,552 --> 00:49:16,092
This really looks fake.

1422
00:49:16,092 --> 00:49:18,322
It looks so fake that I think it's real.

1423
00:49:19,392 --> 00:49:20,392
(Remember the third B-Side hint?)

1424
00:49:20,392 --> 00:49:23,002
(I really can't figure out the inside.)

1425
00:49:23,002 --> 00:49:24,032
(This is...)

1426
00:49:24,032 --> 00:49:26,172
(truly unbalanced.)

1427
00:49:26,172 --> 00:49:28,272
(A very special gimbap restaurant)

1428
00:49:28,572 --> 00:49:30,642
(You have no idea what it is just from looking at it.)

1429
00:49:30,642 --> 00:49:33,242
(Gimbap with mysterious fillings)

1430
00:49:33,412 --> 00:49:36,082
(Is this place real?)

1431
00:49:36,082 --> 00:49:37,942
It's quite pricey though.

1432
00:49:37,942 --> 00:49:39,352
It's more than ten dollars, right?

1433
00:49:39,352 --> 00:49:40,782
There's a gimbap that costs 28 dollars.

1434
00:49:40,782 --> 00:49:44,582
- What? - No gimbap costs 28 dollars.

1435
00:49:45,022 --> 00:49:47,352
"Bottarga gimbap, 28 dollars."

1436
00:49:47,352 --> 00:49:48,352
For gimbap?

1437
00:49:48,522 --> 00:49:50,862
- What kind of gimbap is it? - The price doesn't make sense.

1438
00:49:51,722 --> 00:49:53,732
They sell whiskey and wine.

1439
00:49:53,732 --> 00:49:55,562
Right, they sell whiskey and wine.

1440
00:49:55,862 --> 00:49:58,602
It's a perfect item to make a fake place.

1441
00:49:59,102 --> 00:50:01,402
And the menu too. It's just a piece of paper.

1442
00:50:01,402 --> 00:50:02,402
Right.

1443
00:50:02,402 --> 00:50:05,872
(Everything looks awkward here. Is this place fake?)

1444
00:50:05,872 --> 00:50:07,712
Let's invite the owner.

1445
00:50:08,172 --> 00:50:10,142
- Ma'am. - Ma'am.

1446
00:50:10,682 --> 00:50:11,712
(Peeking)

1447
00:50:11,712 --> 00:50:13,552
- Hello. - Hello.

1448
00:50:13,552 --> 00:50:14,752
- Hello. - Hello.

1449
00:50:14,912 --> 00:50:18,152
- Is this a gimbap place? - Yes, it's a gimbap place.

1450
00:50:18,522 --> 00:50:19,922
They're unique gimbap, right?

1451
00:50:19,922 --> 00:50:23,522
Here, we make gimbap that doesn't exist in the world.

1452
00:50:23,962 --> 00:50:26,692
- "Gimbap that doesn't exist?" - Gimbap you've never seen before.

1453
00:50:26,892 --> 00:50:29,802
Many Koreans say...

1454
00:50:29,802 --> 00:50:31,302
- gimbap is their favorite food. - Right.

1455
00:50:31,302 --> 00:50:33,572
And back then, it was a fun food...

1456
00:50:33,572 --> 00:50:35,772
which we ate every time we went on an outing.

1457
00:50:36,042 --> 00:50:38,502
But nowadays, when someone says they had gimbap for lunch,

1458
00:50:38,502 --> 00:50:39,872
people say, "Why did you make do with it?"

1459
00:50:39,872 --> 00:50:41,372
"You should eat a proper meal when you're busy."

1460
00:50:41,372 --> 00:50:44,212
I felt that it became an unhappy dish.

1461
00:50:44,212 --> 00:50:46,582
(People's perception of gimbap has changed to quick-bite food.)

1462
00:50:46,582 --> 00:50:48,582
"Let's contain the taste globally."

1463
00:50:48,582 --> 00:50:50,882
So when we think about gimbap,

1464
00:50:50,882 --> 00:50:53,952
we'll feel good about it. I wanted to make them feel that way.

1465
00:50:53,952 --> 00:50:54,952
(I wanted people to feel good again when they think about gimbap.)

1466
00:50:55,392 --> 00:50:57,662
On drawing paper of dried laver and rice,

1467
00:50:57,662 --> 00:51:01,792
I wanted to capture the taste of the whole world.

1468
00:51:01,792 --> 00:51:04,102
Everything is different here.

1469
00:51:04,102 --> 00:51:06,932
Nobody thought to eat gimbap with wine,

1470
00:51:06,932 --> 00:51:08,332
so that's different.

1471
00:51:08,332 --> 00:51:11,602
Various ingredients are used for gimbap too.

1472
00:51:12,102 --> 00:51:14,372
If a normal gimbap restaurant has a formula,

1473
00:51:14,372 --> 00:51:17,442
this place breaks all of that.

1474
00:51:17,542 --> 00:51:20,052
(A novel premium gimbap haven)

1475
00:51:20,212 --> 00:51:22,182
Have you heard of bingsu gimbap?

1476
00:51:22,182 --> 00:51:24,252
If you go here, you can see...

1477
00:51:24,252 --> 00:51:25,992
various gimbap you've never seen before.

1478
00:51:26,492 --> 00:51:29,362
Sixth Sense crew, is this place real?

1479
00:51:29,362 --> 00:51:32,322
(Sixth Sense crew, is this place real?)

1480
00:51:32,692 --> 00:51:34,162
How long have you run this place?

1481
00:51:34,162 --> 00:51:37,162
It's been three months since the restaurant...

1482
00:51:37,162 --> 00:51:38,562
looked like this.

1483
00:51:38,562 --> 00:51:40,932
Initially, it was about 20m².

1484
00:51:40,932 --> 00:51:42,372
I started with this one table.

1485
00:51:42,372 --> 00:51:44,142
I only opened once a week on Sundays.

1486
00:51:44,672 --> 00:51:46,172
- Only on Sundays? - Yes.

1487
00:51:46,412 --> 00:51:47,972
What do you do on weekdays?

1488
00:51:48,372 --> 00:51:50,682
On weekdays... I've been working as a food journalist...

1489
00:51:50,682 --> 00:51:52,482
- for 15 years. - You're a journalist?

1490
00:51:52,482 --> 00:51:54,212
- Yes. - That's why you're a good talker.

1491
00:51:54,212 --> 00:51:56,352
- No wonder. - She explains so smoothly.

1492
00:51:56,352 --> 00:51:59,592
So I work as an interpreter at a food event...

1493
00:51:59,592 --> 00:52:01,422
or a moderator...

1494
00:52:01,422 --> 00:52:03,692
- for food conferences. - But...

1495
00:52:03,692 --> 00:52:04,762
(She works for many food-related events.)

1496
00:52:04,762 --> 00:52:06,832
Her vocalization and tone make me think...

1497
00:52:06,832 --> 00:52:10,602
she's either an actress or a voice actress.

1498
00:52:10,602 --> 00:52:12,132
(She's an actress, not a food journalist?)

1499
00:52:12,132 --> 00:52:16,102
I can't match gimbap with wine.

1500
00:52:16,102 --> 00:52:17,102
Right?

1501
00:52:17,102 --> 00:52:18,602
But when you think about...

1502
00:52:18,602 --> 00:52:20,072
the first time people started drinking wine,

1503
00:52:20,072 --> 00:52:21,742
people searched for wine...

1504
00:52:21,742 --> 00:52:24,612
that go well with kimchi stew and braised beef short ribs.

1505
00:52:24,612 --> 00:52:26,982
And we eat gimbap every day,

1506
00:52:26,982 --> 00:52:29,652
so why didn't people think to have wine with it?

1507
00:52:29,652 --> 00:52:31,422
I don't think she's the owner.

1508
00:52:32,022 --> 00:52:34,752
But it is the kind of idea journalists would have.

1509
00:52:34,922 --> 00:52:36,722
Please go ahead and prepare.

1510
00:52:36,722 --> 00:52:37,822
Sure.

1511
00:52:38,992 --> 00:52:41,562
She talks so smoothly, but...

1512
00:52:41,562 --> 00:52:43,462
- It sounded like she prepared it. - It's so...

1513
00:52:44,532 --> 00:52:45,532
This is so hard.

1514
00:52:46,602 --> 00:52:50,602
And the kitchen looks so unused.

1515
00:52:50,742 --> 00:52:51,742
Really?

1516
00:52:51,742 --> 00:52:54,212
(The kitchen looks too unused and clean?)

1517
00:52:54,212 --> 00:52:56,312
And it's not a kitchen for gimbap.

1518
00:52:56,312 --> 00:52:59,582
The pot is way too fancy for gimbap.

1519
00:53:00,052 --> 00:53:05,582
(Why are there high-end pots in a gimbap restaurant?)

1520
00:53:05,622 --> 00:53:07,922
The owner can hear us, but she's pretending she can't.

1521
00:53:07,922 --> 00:53:10,262
(Caught)

1522
00:53:10,392 --> 00:53:11,392
We're sorry. We need to...

1523
00:53:11,392 --> 00:53:12,792
- find the fake place. - I'm enjoying your conversation.

1524
00:53:12,792 --> 00:53:14,532
- It's just how the show is like. - It's the show.

1525
00:53:14,932 --> 00:53:17,232
But she's definitely skilled...

1526
00:53:17,232 --> 00:53:19,032
in making gimbap.

1527
00:53:19,602 --> 00:53:21,402
She's very proficient.

1528
00:53:21,972 --> 00:53:23,972
If she brought the food in from outside,

1529
00:53:23,972 --> 00:53:25,942
I was going to become suspicious, but she's cooking on the spot.

1530
00:53:25,942 --> 00:53:27,812
Exactly.

1531
00:53:27,812 --> 00:53:29,272
In that case,

1532
00:53:29,272 --> 00:53:32,042
the owners who cooked in front of us...

1533
00:53:32,042 --> 00:53:33,282
were the 1st and the 3rd.

1534
00:53:33,282 --> 00:53:35,212
The second restaurant brought out the food from the inside.

1535
00:53:35,482 --> 00:53:38,152
This is what I'm talking about.

1536
00:53:38,552 --> 00:53:39,752
They kept bringing out the food...

1537
00:53:39,752 --> 00:53:40,952
- from the inside. - They brought it from the inside.

1538
00:53:41,092 --> 00:53:45,222
She's showing us the process here, and so did the mini food owner.

1539
00:53:45,592 --> 00:53:47,832
(At the 1st and 3rd restaurants,)

1540
00:53:47,832 --> 00:53:50,402
(the owners cooked right in front of the members.)

1541
00:53:50,802 --> 00:53:52,502
(Could the 2nd restaurant be fake...)

1542
00:53:52,502 --> 00:53:55,372
(since they didn't get to see the cooking?)

1543
00:53:55,572 --> 00:53:59,142
Still, a gimbap here is like 28 dollars...

1544
00:53:59,372 --> 00:54:01,342
or 23 dollars. That doesn't make any sense.

1545
00:54:01,342 --> 00:54:03,812
The gimbap here is pretty pricey.

1546
00:54:04,312 --> 00:54:06,452
Yes. But if you think about it,

1547
00:54:06,682 --> 00:54:09,252
when fine dining first emerged in Korea,

1548
00:54:09,252 --> 00:54:11,822
they served about 3 to 5 dishes...

1549
00:54:11,822 --> 00:54:13,492
for roughly 30 to 50 dollars.

1550
00:54:13,722 --> 00:54:15,692
Even back then, people would say,

1551
00:54:15,692 --> 00:54:17,162
"You can have doenjang jjigae for five dollars at other places."

1552
00:54:17,162 --> 00:54:18,362
"Why would you pay 30 or 50 dollars for this?"

1553
00:54:18,362 --> 00:54:19,632
- We can't win an argument with her. - That's what they said.

1554
00:54:19,632 --> 00:54:22,092
Still, the things that started back then are continuing to grow.

1555
00:54:22,092 --> 00:54:24,602
As Korean cuisine is going global and modern.

1556
00:54:24,602 --> 00:54:26,572
Say one wrong word to her, and we're done for.

1557
00:54:27,202 --> 00:54:29,072
- She's so articulate. - Don't mess with her.

1558
00:54:29,072 --> 00:54:30,842
It feels like she's saying, "You can never beat me."

1559
00:54:30,842 --> 00:54:33,172
Say one wrong thing to her and...

1560
00:54:33,372 --> 00:54:34,512
I'm starting to feel sorry.

1561
00:54:34,512 --> 00:54:35,512
- You'll be done for. - Right.

1562
00:54:35,512 --> 00:54:38,382
And she keeps saying, "If you think about it."

1563
00:54:38,982 --> 00:54:41,382
(If you think about it...)

1564
00:54:41,582 --> 00:54:43,452
I thought to myself, "Maybe we weren't thinking."

1565
00:54:43,452 --> 00:54:44,782
(Brainwashed)

1566
00:54:44,782 --> 00:54:45,782
(Spitting out the water)

1567
00:54:45,782 --> 00:54:48,292
- As if she were waiting for this. - We were being thoughtless.

1568
00:54:48,852 --> 00:54:50,262
We have to think before we ask anything.

1569
00:54:50,622 --> 00:54:51,922
Think before you talk.

1570
00:54:51,922 --> 00:54:53,092
(Today's takeaway: Think before you speak.)

1571
00:54:53,362 --> 00:54:54,532
My goodness.

1572
00:54:54,792 --> 00:54:56,632
(Amazed)

1573
00:54:56,632 --> 00:54:58,632
- What's this? There's a corn dog. - Gosh, this is...

1574
00:54:59,262 --> 00:55:01,102
So we can have wine with this.

1575
00:55:01,532 --> 00:55:03,102
- I just sounded awkward, right? - This is tteokbokki.

1576
00:55:03,572 --> 00:55:04,842
- Why am I being awkward? - Let me take the tray.

1577
00:55:04,842 --> 00:55:06,442
I thought you were starring in a new TV drama.

1578
00:55:07,042 --> 00:55:08,412
- I was being really... - After you said that,

1579
00:55:08,412 --> 00:55:09,412
- I was really surprised by myself. - you were surprised by yourself.

1580
00:55:09,412 --> 00:55:11,442
"That's right. We can have wine."

1581
00:55:11,442 --> 00:55:12,582
(It's hard to say a word today.)

1582
00:55:12,582 --> 00:55:14,052
- This is good. - Let's dig in.

1583
00:55:14,052 --> 00:55:15,212
Let's eat.

1584
00:55:16,582 --> 00:55:17,622
Look at the detail.

1585
00:55:17,622 --> 00:55:19,182
- This is beautiful. - Why do you always...

1586
00:55:19,182 --> 00:55:20,522
This is actually nice.

1587
00:55:22,092 --> 00:55:23,192
This is so cute.

1588
00:55:23,192 --> 00:55:24,592
This is far too neat and refined...

1589
00:55:24,592 --> 00:55:26,632
to be something put together in a hurry.

1590
00:55:26,892 --> 00:55:27,962
(Tteokbokki gimbap with the taste of tteokbokki)

1591
00:55:27,962 --> 00:55:30,262
How could she go this far?

1592
00:55:30,262 --> 00:55:31,602
(Tteokbokki gimbap with the taste of tteokbokki)

1593
00:55:31,932 --> 00:55:33,502
(New Year's gimbap with rice cake soup packed inside)

1594
00:55:33,502 --> 00:55:34,732
This is insane.

1595
00:55:36,542 --> 00:55:37,602
(They start off with a big bite of the delicious-looking gimbap.)

1596
00:55:37,602 --> 00:55:38,702
Can I try it?

1597
00:55:39,512 --> 00:55:40,642
(Exclaiming)

1598
00:55:40,642 --> 00:55:42,342
- This is... - Start with the delicious one.

1599
00:55:42,842 --> 00:55:43,912
- Squid? - Before you eat,

1600
00:55:43,912 --> 00:55:45,312
let me explain the gimbap.

1601
00:55:45,312 --> 00:55:46,312
Okay.

1602
00:55:46,312 --> 00:55:48,812
The one you're eating now is the New Year's gimbap.

1603
00:55:48,812 --> 00:55:49,822
- New Year? - New Year?

1604
00:55:49,822 --> 00:55:51,452
- Tteokguk. - That's right.

1605
00:55:51,582 --> 00:55:52,992
It's filled with dumpling filling, sliced rice cakes,

1606
00:55:52,992 --> 00:55:54,822
- beef, and eggs. - Seriously?

1607
00:55:55,992 --> 00:55:58,962
If you take a bite and sip the broth right after,

1608
00:55:58,962 --> 00:56:00,662
- it turns into tteokguk. - It feels just like the holidays.

1609
00:56:00,662 --> 00:56:01,892
(Tteokguk is complete inside your mouth.)

1610
00:56:02,332 --> 00:56:05,102
This is a gimbap I make a lot during the New Year.

1611
00:56:05,102 --> 00:56:06,572
- This is tteokguk. - Since today marks...

1612
00:56:06,672 --> 00:56:07,902
our first meeting,

1613
00:56:07,902 --> 00:56:09,472
- I prepared it especially for you. - This is good.

1614
00:56:09,472 --> 00:56:11,102
- Awesome. - This tastes...

1615
00:56:11,402 --> 00:56:13,312
just like homemade...

1616
00:56:13,312 --> 00:56:15,612
- tteokguk. - This is just like tteokguk.

1617
00:56:15,612 --> 00:56:17,812
- It's a brilliant idea. - My goodness.

1618
00:56:18,042 --> 00:56:19,552
It's like eating rice in tteokguk.

1619
00:56:19,552 --> 00:56:20,612
- Exactly. - Right.

1620
00:56:20,612 --> 00:56:21,952
- There are rice cakes in it. - Yes.

1621
00:56:21,952 --> 00:56:23,982
(A bowl of tteokguk reimagined as gimbap)

1622
00:56:24,552 --> 00:56:27,852
(How about New Year's gimbap instead of tteokguk this New Year?)

1623
00:56:28,092 --> 00:56:29,252
My goodness. This is...

1624
00:56:30,222 --> 00:56:31,492
beyond my imagination.

1625
00:56:31,492 --> 00:56:33,092
- I know. - This isn't some cheap,

1626
00:56:33,092 --> 00:56:34,632
- clumsy combo. - Right.

1627
00:56:34,632 --> 00:56:36,802
- Everything blends in so well. - I agree.

1628
00:56:36,802 --> 00:56:39,232
- This is so good. - She cooks so well.

1629
00:56:39,632 --> 00:56:41,902
- It tastes like healthy gimbap. - Yes.

1630
00:56:41,902 --> 00:56:43,072
- Right? - With cheese.

1631
00:56:43,272 --> 00:56:44,502
There's more?

1632
00:56:44,502 --> 00:56:46,002
This is bingsu gimbap,

1633
00:56:46,002 --> 00:56:49,012
which you can enjoy like a dessert after your meal.

1634
00:56:49,012 --> 00:56:50,942
(Bingsu gimbap)

1635
00:56:50,942 --> 00:56:53,282
"Couldn't a gimbap place make desserts too?"

1636
00:56:53,282 --> 00:56:54,982
It started with this thought.

1637
00:56:55,252 --> 00:56:56,922
If you think about old-school bingsu,

1638
00:56:56,922 --> 00:56:59,352
it's a simple dish with red beans,

1639
00:56:59,352 --> 00:57:02,522
bean powder, rice cakes, and condensed milk.

1640
00:57:02,522 --> 00:57:03,592
(Inspired by classic, simple red bean bingsu)

1641
00:57:03,922 --> 00:57:06,532
I captured those ingredients inside the gimbap.

1642
00:57:06,532 --> 00:57:08,432
Since gimbap already has rice,

1643
00:57:08,432 --> 00:57:09,932
I figured you'd get that chewy rice-cake texture...

1644
00:57:09,932 --> 00:57:12,302
even without adding actual rice cakes.

1645
00:57:12,432 --> 00:57:14,172
I stuffed the gimbap with a mixture...

1646
00:57:14,172 --> 00:57:15,632
of cereal and sweet red beans.

1647
00:57:15,632 --> 00:57:17,572
(Rolling up a gimbap packed with bingsu toppings)

1648
00:57:18,202 --> 00:57:20,372
(An unmistakable gimbap visual)

1649
00:57:20,372 --> 00:57:22,572
It's coated with cereal flakes...

1650
00:57:22,572 --> 00:57:24,612
and powdered soybean.

1651
00:57:25,012 --> 00:57:27,752
(A burst of nutty aroma)

1652
00:57:28,482 --> 00:57:31,222
(Finished with adorable puffed rice on top)

1653
00:57:31,222 --> 00:57:33,492
If you coat the gimbap with as much of that as possible,

1654
00:57:33,492 --> 00:57:35,722
you can enjoy a delightfully crunchy gimbap.

1655
00:57:36,562 --> 00:57:38,722
So this is the bingsu gimbap, right?

1656
00:57:40,032 --> 00:57:41,362
This is interesting.

1657
00:57:41,832 --> 00:57:43,602
This is so detailed.

1658
00:57:45,032 --> 00:57:46,572
- If you like this kind of stuff... - It's so good.

1659
00:57:46,572 --> 00:57:48,702
(It's so good.)

1660
00:57:48,772 --> 00:57:51,742
I thought she just wrapped bingsu in seaweed, but that's not it.

1661
00:57:52,402 --> 00:57:54,972
It captures the actual taste of bingsu in a gimbap.

1662
00:57:54,972 --> 00:57:56,342
It recreates the flavor.

1663
00:57:57,082 --> 00:57:58,812
This is delicious too.

1664
00:57:58,812 --> 00:57:59,812
(Exclaiming)

1665
00:58:00,012 --> 00:58:02,112
- It tastes just like sirutteok. - That's right.

1666
00:58:02,752 --> 00:58:03,782
This is so good.

1667
00:58:03,782 --> 00:58:05,582
It nails the texture,

1668
00:58:05,582 --> 00:58:07,552
like eating the red beans and rice cakes...

1669
00:58:07,552 --> 00:58:09,352
from bingsu together!

1670
00:58:09,352 --> 00:58:11,092
- With the powdered soybean. - It's so delicious.

1671
00:58:11,092 --> 00:58:12,392
I was worried it would all just melt,

1672
00:58:12,392 --> 00:58:14,062
- but that wasn't the case at all. - It's just amazing.

1673
00:58:14,262 --> 00:58:15,362
My gosh, this is...

1674
00:58:15,862 --> 00:58:19,362
The owner seems to understand ingredients.

1675
00:58:19,362 --> 00:58:20,372
Totally.

1676
00:58:20,372 --> 00:58:22,602
You have to grasp the flavor balance to pull this off.

1677
00:58:22,602 --> 00:58:23,672
Of course.

1678
00:58:24,272 --> 00:58:25,372
Wait, but...

1679
00:58:25,802 --> 00:58:27,542
is this actually real?

1680
00:58:27,742 --> 00:58:29,272
(Suddenly spiraling into confusion)

1681
00:58:29,442 --> 00:58:31,112
Having tasted it,

1682
00:58:31,412 --> 00:58:33,682
- I think it's real. - I agree.

1683
00:58:33,952 --> 00:58:35,452
I can see a deep understanding of food...

1684
00:58:35,452 --> 00:58:36,752
and plenty of experience.

1685
00:58:36,752 --> 00:58:39,722
As a journalist, she must've done a ton of reporting.

1686
00:58:40,022 --> 00:58:41,652
Without that kind of background,

1687
00:58:42,392 --> 00:58:43,822
it'd be hard to create this.

1688
00:58:44,022 --> 00:58:45,722
I hear the production crew of Sixth Sense...

1689
00:58:45,722 --> 00:58:48,262
prepares things three months in advance.

1690
00:58:48,892 --> 00:58:49,932
If she practiced...

1691
00:58:49,932 --> 00:58:52,432
Coincidentally, she said it was remodeled three months ago.

1692
00:58:52,762 --> 00:58:53,972
- Exactly. - Right?

1693
00:58:54,302 --> 00:58:55,972
- That timeline is way too tight. - You're right.

1694
00:58:56,202 --> 00:58:58,372
- Always... - Woo Jae, we're talking.

1695
00:58:58,372 --> 00:59:00,272
The scraping sound is too loud.

1696
00:59:00,572 --> 00:59:01,572
I'm sorry.

1697
00:59:01,912 --> 00:59:04,612
Everything blends together so well,

1698
00:59:04,612 --> 00:59:06,582
but did this owner really create all of this?

1699
00:59:06,582 --> 00:59:07,612
(Suspicious)

1700
00:59:07,612 --> 00:59:10,152
- I have many doubts. - It's so tricky at the end.

1701
00:59:10,152 --> 00:59:11,882
(It's getting confusing at the end.)

1702
00:59:11,882 --> 00:59:12,982
It's so confusing.

1703
00:59:13,422 --> 00:59:15,652
- Let's just go. - It's easy if only one is real.

1704
00:59:15,652 --> 00:59:17,322
It's hard to choose since only one of them is fake.

1705
00:59:17,322 --> 00:59:19,162
(A gimbap place that doesn't exist elsewhere)

1706
00:59:19,422 --> 00:59:21,262
Today is exceptionally tricky.

1707
00:59:21,692 --> 00:59:23,732
It's way too hard. Now I'm...

1708
00:59:23,732 --> 00:59:26,102
But what makes me suspicious of the last place is...

1709
00:59:26,532 --> 00:59:29,402
that she seems focused on selling wine,

1710
00:59:29,502 --> 00:59:31,142
yet gimbap is the only thing on the menu.

1711
00:59:31,142 --> 00:59:32,642
- That doesn't add up. - I know.

1712
00:59:32,772 --> 00:59:36,142
And before leaving the last place after the shoot,

1713
00:59:36,142 --> 00:59:37,212
(Before he left the restaurant)

1714
00:59:37,212 --> 00:59:38,912
I looked for napkins to wipe my mouth,

1715
00:59:38,912 --> 00:59:40,212
and there weren't any napkins...

1716
00:59:40,212 --> 00:59:41,512
- in the restaurant at all. - There were no napkins?

1717
00:59:41,982 --> 00:59:43,682
How is that even possible?

1718
00:59:44,182 --> 00:59:45,522
Are there no napkins here?

1719
00:59:46,592 --> 00:59:48,622
(There are no napkins, which are a restaurant essential.)

1720
00:59:48,622 --> 00:59:49,992
A restaurant with no napkins?

1721
00:59:50,592 --> 00:59:52,562
Eun I. At first, I thought,

1722
00:59:52,562 --> 00:59:54,592
- "Is it okay to be this easy?" - I know. I felt the same way.

1723
00:59:54,592 --> 00:59:55,662
But this is too hard.

1724
00:59:55,662 --> 00:59:57,162
The difficulty is brutal.

1725
00:59:57,162 --> 00:59:59,972
Anyway, Woo Jae, thanks for coming today.

1726
01:00:00,132 --> 01:00:01,502
- Is he leaving here? - No.

1727
01:00:01,502 --> 01:00:03,572
I just want him to stay quiet.

1728
01:00:04,342 --> 01:00:05,842
- We are... - This is driving me crazy.

1729
01:00:05,842 --> 01:00:08,072
There's nothing about Woo Jae we don't already know.

1730
01:00:08,372 --> 01:00:11,412
I should check who the MCs are before agreeing to guest on a show.

1731
01:00:11,412 --> 01:00:12,912
- You didn't check? - No.

1732
01:00:12,912 --> 01:00:14,282
I just thought they were familiar faces...

1733
01:00:14,282 --> 01:00:16,012
and agreed to come without thinking much.

1734
01:00:16,012 --> 01:00:17,282
(Never let familiarity fool you into reckless decisions.)

1735
01:00:17,552 --> 01:00:20,352
(Forget I even said anything.)

1736
01:00:20,352 --> 01:00:22,052
(Watching something carefully)

1737
01:00:22,052 --> 01:00:23,762
Over here, it says "Small Is the Answer..."

1738
01:00:23,762 --> 01:00:24,992
on the bus.

1739
01:00:25,092 --> 01:00:26,992
- What? - "Small Is the Answer."

1740
01:00:26,992 --> 01:00:28,792
- That's me. - That's right.

1741
01:00:28,792 --> 01:00:30,032
Is this a clue?

1742
01:00:30,032 --> 01:00:31,562
Small is the answer?

1743
01:00:31,802 --> 01:00:33,802
- Hey. - This is unbelievable.

1744
01:00:33,802 --> 01:00:34,932
Isn't this a clue?

1745
01:00:34,932 --> 01:00:36,072
- No way. - No.

1746
01:00:36,072 --> 01:00:37,442
It's just an ordinary bus.

1747
01:00:37,442 --> 01:00:38,442
We shouldn't get too obsessed...

1748
01:00:38,442 --> 01:00:39,872
- with B-Side. - Is that so?

1749
01:00:39,872 --> 01:00:41,872
This is just a random passing bus.

1750
01:00:41,872 --> 01:00:43,442
It's a coincidence.

1751
01:00:43,542 --> 01:00:45,812
Why does it keep sticking right beside us, then?

1752
01:00:45,812 --> 01:00:48,082
But can two vehicles drive side by side like this?

1753
01:00:48,082 --> 01:00:49,082
- Seriously... - What?

1754
01:00:49,082 --> 01:00:50,722
Did they charter a bus?

1755
01:00:51,022 --> 01:00:52,182
An entire bus?

1756
01:00:52,382 --> 01:00:53,922
This is absurd.

1757
01:00:54,252 --> 01:00:56,522
It's running away now that it got caught!

1758
01:00:57,622 --> 01:00:59,022
If small is the answer,

1759
01:00:59,022 --> 01:01:00,532
does that mean the first restaurant is the fake?

1760
01:01:00,532 --> 01:01:01,532
- Yes. - Yes.

1761
01:01:01,532 --> 01:01:02,662
That has to be the answer.

1762
01:01:02,662 --> 01:01:04,002
No, that can't be it.

1763
01:01:04,002 --> 01:01:05,362
(It seems so real.)

1764
01:01:06,302 --> 01:01:08,672
(This isn't a place to get a full meal.)

1765
01:01:08,832 --> 01:01:10,702
(An experiential restaurant)

1766
01:01:10,702 --> 01:01:12,902
(A miniature Korean omakase where you enjoy bite-sized dishes)

1767
01:01:13,272 --> 01:01:15,742
- The food was way too tiny. - Right.

1768
01:01:16,342 --> 01:01:20,182
(Cute little dishes that felt just like playing house)

1769
01:01:20,382 --> 01:01:21,982
(It sure makes...)

1770
01:01:21,982 --> 01:01:25,682
(the experience fun.)

1771
01:01:26,022 --> 01:01:29,092
(An experience that melts the heart)

1772
01:01:29,152 --> 01:01:30,222
(No. 1 Miniature Korean omakase)

1773
01:01:30,222 --> 01:01:31,762
The food was really good.

1774
01:01:31,762 --> 01:01:33,732
(No. 1 Miniature Korean omakase)

1775
01:01:34,392 --> 01:01:36,732
Would they really charter an entire bus just for this show?

1776
01:01:37,832 --> 01:01:40,272
I think that's going to be the key factor today.

1777
01:01:40,272 --> 01:01:41,672
(Did the bus just happen to pass by? Or was it the B-Side?)

1778
01:01:41,902 --> 01:01:43,702
This is driving me crazy.

1779
01:01:43,702 --> 01:01:44,902
This is too hard.

1780
01:01:45,102 --> 01:01:46,942
(Time to make the final decision)

1781
01:01:47,242 --> 01:01:49,172
Shouldn't we go with number one on this?

1782
01:01:49,172 --> 01:01:50,312
(Choosing No. 1 right away?)

1783
01:01:50,612 --> 01:01:52,612
No? Are we going with number three?

1784
01:01:52,812 --> 01:01:54,752
(My head is killing me.)

1785
01:01:55,012 --> 01:01:56,922
Don't get me wrong.

1786
01:01:56,922 --> 01:01:58,622
I think it might be number two.

1787
01:01:59,422 --> 01:02:01,992
That's the only place where they cooked inside.

1788
01:02:03,562 --> 01:02:05,192
(A beef bone soup course meal...)

1789
01:02:05,192 --> 01:02:07,792
(reimagining grandma's homemade cooking)

1790
01:02:08,162 --> 01:02:10,602
(A transformation of beef bone soup)

1791
01:02:11,002 --> 01:02:14,002
(A diverse course meal created around beef bone soup)

1792
01:02:14,502 --> 01:02:15,672
(For something simmered for 40 hours,)

1793
01:02:15,672 --> 01:02:16,872
(there's no beef bone soup smell at all.)

1794
01:02:16,872 --> 01:02:18,202
(This place is the fake!)

1795
01:02:18,202 --> 01:02:19,572
(A fake made by mixing various store-bought broths?)

1796
01:02:20,242 --> 01:02:22,242
(A fake broth created by the production crew...)

1797
01:02:22,242 --> 01:02:23,842
(using store-bought bases?)

1798
01:02:24,082 --> 01:02:26,812
(No. 2 Korea's first beef bone soup course meal)

1799
01:02:27,352 --> 01:02:29,682
All right. Let's give that bus one last piece of thought.

1800
01:02:29,682 --> 01:02:32,782
Could the phrase "Small Is the Answer" really be...

1801
01:02:32,782 --> 01:02:34,322
- the final hint thrown at us? - It's the clue.

1802
01:02:34,392 --> 01:02:36,792
Come to think of it, number one is the only small thing.

1803
01:02:37,192 --> 01:02:38,292
You're right.

1804
01:02:39,462 --> 01:02:41,162
We haven't seen anything other than the bus.

1805
01:02:41,162 --> 01:02:43,632
(That bus is the only thing that could be a B-Side clue.)

1806
01:02:44,132 --> 01:02:45,502
All right then.

1807
01:02:46,132 --> 01:02:47,672
In times like this, let's play rock-paper-scissors...

1808
01:02:47,672 --> 01:02:49,732
- and have the winner decide. - I want to lose.

1809
01:02:50,042 --> 01:02:51,072
All right.

1810
01:02:51,072 --> 01:02:52,302
- Rock-paper-scissors. - Rock-paper-scissors.

1811
01:02:53,542 --> 01:02:54,812
(Rock)

1812
01:02:54,812 --> 01:02:55,812
(The only one who threw paper)

1813
01:02:55,812 --> 01:02:57,382
(Yu Jae Seok's solo victory)

1814
01:02:57,512 --> 01:02:58,882
- Oh, dear. - Oh, no.

1815
01:02:58,882 --> 01:02:59,882
(Keeping their distance)

1816
01:02:59,882 --> 01:03:00,882
Jae Seok.

1817
01:03:01,382 --> 01:03:03,582
- We didn't plan this. - What just happened?

1818
01:03:04,152 --> 01:03:05,152
(We're innocent.)

1819
01:03:05,152 --> 01:03:07,192
- What just happened? - You suggested this.

1820
01:03:07,192 --> 01:03:08,322
How would we plan this?

1821
01:03:08,552 --> 01:03:09,822
How did this happen?

1822
01:03:09,822 --> 01:03:10,822
(Headache)

1823
01:03:10,822 --> 01:03:11,922
How is this...

1824
01:03:12,922 --> 01:03:14,432
(She's just glad that it didn't happen to her.)

1825
01:03:14,432 --> 01:03:15,492
- All right. - All right.

1826
01:03:16,332 --> 01:03:17,902
Given the way things played out,

1827
01:03:17,902 --> 01:03:19,432
going with number one is the right call.

1828
01:03:19,562 --> 01:03:21,172
If we changed our choice here, we might regret it.

1829
01:03:21,172 --> 01:03:22,172
- Sure. - You're right.

1830
01:03:22,172 --> 01:03:23,472
You have a point.

1831
01:03:23,472 --> 01:03:25,142
My choice is number one.

1832
01:03:25,672 --> 01:03:26,812
- Okay. Let's go with number one. - Let's go.

1833
01:03:26,812 --> 01:03:28,042
That's the only option.

1834
01:03:28,042 --> 01:03:29,272
- Please. - All right.

1835
01:03:29,272 --> 01:03:30,312
This is...

1836
01:03:30,982 --> 01:03:32,242
(The vast world of Korean cuisine)

1837
01:03:32,242 --> 01:03:34,582
Korean food covers way too broad a spectrum.

1838
01:03:35,482 --> 01:03:36,752
It was really good.

1839
01:03:37,722 --> 01:03:40,322
(Can Korean cuisine really go this far?)

1840
01:03:41,122 --> 01:03:42,522
- No! - No!

1841
01:03:42,992 --> 01:03:44,562
Failing to guess this is not acceptable.

1842
01:03:45,362 --> 01:03:46,492
(Which one is the only fake?)

1843
01:03:46,492 --> 01:03:48,092
This is too tricky.

1844
01:03:48,092 --> 01:03:49,492
I just can't get it right.

1845
01:03:49,492 --> 01:03:50,502
Please.

1846
01:03:51,062 --> 01:03:52,102
Let's go.

1847
01:03:52,532 --> 01:03:53,872
(Which one is the only fake?)

1848
01:03:53,872 --> 01:03:55,132
Which one is the fake?

1849
01:03:55,832 --> 01:03:58,842
- Let's go. - It has to be number one.

1850
01:03:58,942 --> 01:04:00,012
All right.

1851
01:04:00,012 --> 01:04:02,072
It will be crazy if it turns out to be number three.

1852
01:04:02,172 --> 01:04:04,412
- All right! - Please.

1853
01:04:04,412 --> 01:04:06,482
(They want to get it right so badly today.)

1854
01:04:07,012 --> 01:04:10,022
(It has to be No. 1!)

1855
01:04:10,022 --> 01:04:12,182
(Please stop on No. 1!)

1856
01:04:12,182 --> 01:04:13,222
Which one?

1857
01:04:13,222 --> 01:04:16,022
(Which one is the only fake?)

1858
01:04:16,128 --> 01:04:18,858
(It has to be No. 1!)

1859
01:04:18,858 --> 01:04:20,298
(Please stop on No. 1!)

1860
01:04:20,298 --> 01:04:21,598
- What? - Which one?

1861
01:04:21,668 --> 01:04:24,868
(Which one is the only fake?)

1862
01:04:27,238 --> 01:04:28,908
- Okay! - Is it number one?

1863
01:04:29,008 --> 01:04:30,678
Did we get it right?

1864
01:04:31,238 --> 01:04:32,778
(It's correct!)

1865
01:04:32,778 --> 01:04:34,448
(High-pitched shrieks of joy)

1866
01:04:34,448 --> 01:04:36,148
Did we get it right?

1867
01:04:36,148 --> 01:04:37,418
(We did it!)

1868
01:04:37,618 --> 01:04:39,078
I'm so relieved.

1869
01:04:39,248 --> 01:04:42,218
- That's such a relief. - We did it.

1870
01:04:42,788 --> 01:04:43,958
Hang on.

1871
01:04:44,518 --> 01:04:47,128
Did you really look down on us and drop that hint right next to us?

1872
01:04:47,958 --> 01:04:51,198
You got caught!

1873
01:04:51,258 --> 01:04:53,728
You literally had that bus driving right alongside us.

1874
01:04:54,628 --> 01:04:57,338
(Why was the miniature Korean omakase today's fake?)

1875
01:04:57,668 --> 01:04:58,938
(I can't eat anymore.)

1876
01:04:59,138 --> 01:05:01,568
(Joo Woo Jae with his tiny appetite)

1877
01:05:02,108 --> 01:05:04,078
(That's incredible. How do you eat all of this?)

1878
01:05:04,108 --> 01:05:05,508
(How tall are you?)

1879
01:05:05,508 --> 01:05:07,048
The one sitting there is Kyeong Hwan, right?

1880
01:05:08,008 --> 01:05:09,048
(Create a miniature food restaurant just for them!)

1881
01:05:09,048 --> 01:05:10,748
"Create a miniature food restaurant?"

1882
01:05:11,018 --> 01:05:13,718
Casting today's guests was a hint in itself.

1883
01:05:13,718 --> 01:05:14,748
You're right.

1884
01:05:14,888 --> 01:05:16,618
Small appetite and short height.

1885
01:05:17,418 --> 01:05:19,618
- That was the clue. - Small appetite and short height.

1886
01:05:19,688 --> 01:05:21,558
That's how they picked the keyword.

1887
01:05:21,688 --> 01:05:23,158
(Then what is the identity of the miniature Korean omakase?)

1888
01:05:23,158 --> 01:05:24,398
What was this place for?

1889
01:05:24,898 --> 01:05:25,928
(It was an ordinary cooking studio.)

1890
01:05:25,928 --> 01:05:27,668
So it was actually a cooking studio.

1891
01:05:27,668 --> 01:05:29,568
No wonder it felt like a studio.

1892
01:05:30,498 --> 01:05:33,508
(Bringing in tiny, adorable props)

1893
01:05:33,738 --> 01:05:35,238
(A one-of-a-kind miniature Korean omakase is complete.)

1894
01:05:35,238 --> 01:05:37,538
- It's incredible. - It's amazing.

1895
01:05:38,238 --> 01:05:39,308
(Going all the way to Changwon to meet a miniature food expert)

1896
01:05:39,308 --> 01:05:41,508
- That place is our hometown. - Our hometown.

1897
01:05:41,648 --> 01:05:44,518
I'm Eun Myeong Ju, running a YouTube channel...

1898
01:05:44,518 --> 01:05:45,678
for miniature food.

1899
01:05:46,378 --> 01:05:48,488
So she does cook in real life.

1900
01:05:48,488 --> 01:05:49,748
(A miniature expert who loves making tiny food!)

1901
01:05:50,718 --> 01:05:52,018
She doesn't run a restaurant, though.

1902
01:05:52,118 --> 01:05:53,488
(She can pull off Korean, Chinese, and Japanese cuisines.)

1903
01:05:53,488 --> 01:05:55,558
No wonder.

1904
01:05:56,128 --> 01:05:57,758
That's why it tasted so good.

1905
01:05:57,758 --> 01:05:59,228
(She's capable of Western dishes and desserts too.)

1906
01:05:59,628 --> 01:06:01,968
How could she claim she was the building owner...

1907
01:06:01,968 --> 01:06:04,238
- so naturally? - With such a straight face.

1908
01:06:04,238 --> 01:06:05,438
(It felt so natural that they almost fell for it.)

1909
01:06:05,438 --> 01:06:06,768
(Where on earth did they find all these tiny vegetables?)

1910
01:06:06,768 --> 01:06:08,138
What is this?

1911
01:06:08,608 --> 01:06:10,238
When making miniature food,

1912
01:06:10,238 --> 01:06:13,078
I pick and use peppers like this.

1913
01:06:13,078 --> 01:06:14,108
(Baby crops harvested straight from her garden)

1914
01:06:14,108 --> 01:06:16,378
- She only picks the tiny ones. - This part is real.

1915
01:06:16,548 --> 01:06:18,748
Is this all you have now?

1916
01:06:19,018 --> 01:06:21,418
It's a bit less than usual.

1917
01:06:22,488 --> 01:06:25,618
We have six cast members coming.

1918
01:06:25,618 --> 01:06:27,488
Will this be enough ingredients?

1919
01:06:28,128 --> 01:06:29,658
I don't think it will be enough. What do we do?

1920
01:06:29,658 --> 01:06:31,598
- Oh, no. - I'll need more.

1921
01:06:31,598 --> 01:06:33,228
(So we prepared something one month in advance.)

1922
01:06:33,228 --> 01:06:34,298
What?

1923
01:06:34,898 --> 01:06:35,968
(The production crew's do-it-yourself initiative)

1924
01:06:35,968 --> 01:06:38,598
- Seriously? - They grew the vegetables.

1925
01:06:38,868 --> 01:06:39,998
They even went that far?

1926
01:06:39,998 --> 01:06:41,808
- It's really impressive. - It's amazing.

1927
01:06:41,908 --> 01:06:43,208
(Kickstarting the garden inside the editing room)

1928
01:06:43,208 --> 01:06:45,278
Juggling video editing and plant parenting.

1929
01:06:45,578 --> 01:06:47,708
They went through a lot.

1930
01:06:47,948 --> 01:06:49,008
(Grow big and fresh, little vegetables!)

1931
01:06:49,008 --> 01:06:50,278
They must've grown attached to them.

1932
01:06:51,448 --> 01:06:53,078
The crew really went through so much.

1933
01:06:53,078 --> 01:06:54,088
(Were the vegetables grown by the production crew delicious?)

1934
01:06:54,088 --> 01:06:56,518
So we ate perilla leaves grown in the editing room.

1935
01:06:56,518 --> 01:06:58,558
- And we ate editing-room lettuce. - Right.

1936
01:06:58,558 --> 01:06:59,958
The production crew is amazing.

1937
01:06:59,958 --> 01:07:04,428
(We specially prepared them organically for you.)

1938
01:07:04,528 --> 01:07:06,898
(Today's B-Side clue)

1939
01:07:06,898 --> 01:07:08,398
There was only one B-Side clue, right?

1940
01:07:08,598 --> 01:07:10,238
- I didn't see anything. - Me neither.

1941
01:07:10,238 --> 01:07:11,938
(Clues that could have slipped by anytime, anywhere)

1942
01:07:12,568 --> 01:07:14,408
The B-Side clue was the bus.

1943
01:07:14,408 --> 01:07:16,668
I feel like they looked down on us.

1944
01:07:16,878 --> 01:07:18,378
I'm so offended.

1945
01:07:21,478 --> 01:07:22,978
- Backpack. - What?

1946
01:07:22,978 --> 01:07:24,648
- Backpack? - Where?

1947
01:07:25,178 --> 01:07:26,748
What? Where?

1948
01:07:27,688 --> 01:07:28,748
Backpack?

1949
01:07:28,748 --> 01:07:31,418
(The unexpected appearance of a backpack)

1950
01:07:32,258 --> 01:07:33,318
Whose backpack?

1951
01:07:33,688 --> 01:07:34,758
- I didn't see it. - Where?

1952
01:07:34,758 --> 01:07:36,898
(Before the opening this morning when the guests walked in,)

1953
01:07:36,898 --> 01:07:40,468
(didn't you see this backpack passing beside you?)

1954
01:07:40,728 --> 01:07:41,998
How would we see that?

1955
01:07:42,968 --> 01:07:44,398
How would we see that?

1956
01:07:44,898 --> 01:07:47,208
I had no idea.

1957
01:07:47,268 --> 01:07:49,368
(The clue hidden on the LED backpack)

1958
01:07:49,838 --> 01:07:51,638
"Story of a Little Kid."

1959
01:07:51,638 --> 01:07:52,978
(Haha's concert?)

1960
01:07:53,178 --> 01:07:54,778
It says "Little."

1961
01:07:54,778 --> 01:07:56,008
So it was right there all along.

1962
01:07:56,308 --> 01:07:58,878
(Today's B-Side: Backpack)

1963
01:07:59,018 --> 01:08:00,048
Bike.

1964
01:08:00,048 --> 01:08:01,818
- What? Bike? - Bike?

1965
01:08:02,848 --> 01:08:03,958
Was there a bike?

1966
01:08:04,318 --> 01:08:05,758
- Where? - Where was it?

1967
01:08:06,358 --> 01:08:09,758
(Do you remember this man?)

1968
01:08:09,958 --> 01:08:11,928
- Where? - I didn't see him.

1969
01:08:12,228 --> 01:08:13,358
I didn't see him at all.

1970
01:08:14,468 --> 01:08:15,868
As we walked toward the gimbap place?

1971
01:08:15,868 --> 01:08:17,838
I saw him.

1972
01:08:18,098 --> 01:08:19,238
- Really? - Yes.

1973
01:08:19,468 --> 01:08:22,438
There was a bike over...

1974
01:08:22,438 --> 01:08:24,678
- Did you see that? - where the crew was standing.

1975
01:08:24,678 --> 01:08:27,178
- You caught everything. - He saw everything.

1976
01:08:27,178 --> 01:08:30,148
(Did you also take a close look at the flyer he was picking up?)

1977
01:08:30,248 --> 01:08:31,848
(Don't miss even the smallest thing.)

1978
01:08:31,848 --> 01:08:33,478
Small is the answer.

1979
01:08:34,018 --> 01:08:36,248
(Today's B-Side: Bike)

1980
01:08:36,788 --> 01:08:39,188
- Bus. - Yes, we saw the bus.

1981
01:08:39,558 --> 01:08:40,988
Small is the answer.

1982
01:08:43,428 --> 01:08:46,058
Hello, we're the production crew of Sixth Sense.

1983
01:08:46,328 --> 01:08:47,368
Yes, go ahead.

1984
01:08:47,368 --> 01:08:48,868
We want to charter a bus for a shoot...

1985
01:08:48,868 --> 01:08:50,738
and place a clue on it.

1986
01:08:50,738 --> 01:08:52,898
- Yes, you can do that. - Unbelievable.

1987
01:08:53,368 --> 01:08:55,068
(Designing a clue disguised as a public service announcement)

1988
01:08:55,068 --> 01:08:56,268
They went this far?

1989
01:08:56,268 --> 01:08:57,808
(Designing a clue disguised as a public service announcement)

1990
01:08:58,738 --> 01:09:00,208
They attached the ad.

1991
01:09:00,378 --> 01:09:01,778
It's amazing.

1992
01:09:03,078 --> 01:09:04,778
We'll be driving in Lane Two,

1993
01:09:04,778 --> 01:09:07,588
so please keep the bus running alongside us in Lane Three.

1994
01:09:07,648 --> 01:09:09,388
Go.

1995
01:09:10,688 --> 01:09:11,988
(They even ran a rehearsal for 3 hours to nail the timing.)

1996
01:09:11,988 --> 01:09:13,928
They really went through a lot.

1997
01:09:14,488 --> 01:09:16,528
(That's how the B-Side clues were prepared.)

1998
01:09:17,028 --> 01:09:20,798
(They were caught by Woo Jae, who was staring out the window.)

1999
01:09:21,468 --> 01:09:23,668
(They could've brushed it off.)

2000
01:09:23,668 --> 01:09:26,498
(Production crew's thoughts: Please stop.)

2001
01:09:26,838 --> 01:09:28,968
(The persistent man with the relentlessly sharp sixth sense)

2002
01:09:29,268 --> 01:09:31,308
(Being persuaded by Woo Jae)

2003
01:09:31,538 --> 01:09:34,108
If we make a sudden turn here, they'll get more suspicious,

2004
01:09:34,108 --> 01:09:35,208
so let's just keep following them.

2005
01:09:36,048 --> 01:09:37,118
We're doomed.

2006
01:09:38,248 --> 01:09:42,748
(And that's how today's B-Side was ruined.)

2007
01:09:42,918 --> 01:09:45,218
Today's heroes are Jae Seok and Eun I.

2008
01:09:45,558 --> 01:09:47,028
It's because they didn't talk to me.

2009
01:09:48,088 --> 01:09:49,458
So I just kept staring outside.

2010
01:09:49,458 --> 01:09:50,828
You blankly stared out.

2011
01:09:50,828 --> 01:09:51,998
That's when I found it.

2012
01:09:53,228 --> 01:09:54,968
That's unbelievable.

2013
01:09:55,798 --> 01:09:56,828
(The final B-Side clue they didn't catch until the end)

2014
01:09:56,828 --> 01:09:58,568
- Was there more? - What was it?

2015
01:09:58,768 --> 01:10:01,068
(Right on the vehicle you all rode in)

2016
01:10:02,768 --> 01:10:03,838
(No. 1 is the fake.)

2017
01:10:03,838 --> 01:10:05,678
- Right on the van. - It says number one is the fake.

2018
01:10:05,678 --> 01:10:06,708
(The last B-Side clue hidden on the vehicle they rode)

2019
01:10:06,708 --> 01:10:09,148
- Something was attached to the van. - How would we know that?

2020
01:10:09,448 --> 01:10:11,718
(Laughing)

2021
01:10:11,848 --> 01:10:13,218
So that's why something was...

2022
01:10:13,218 --> 01:10:14,418
- covering the sunroof! - Right. There was something.

2023
01:10:14,418 --> 01:10:16,118
I thought the production crew was just being considerate.

2024
01:10:16,118 --> 01:10:17,118
It's hot because of...

2025
01:10:17,118 --> 01:10:18,918
- the upper window. - Right.

2026
01:10:19,488 --> 01:10:21,328
If you had tried, you could've seen it.

2027
01:10:21,328 --> 01:10:22,828
I saw only a tiny part of it.

2028
01:10:23,628 --> 01:10:25,558
This place is fake.

2029
01:10:25,558 --> 01:10:28,128
(No. 1 miniature Korean omakase is the fake!)

2030
01:10:28,128 --> 01:10:29,368
I'm glad we got it right.

2031
01:10:29,368 --> 01:10:31,398
(We got it right today!)

2032
01:10:31,468 --> 01:10:33,168
(Yes!)

2033
01:10:33,298 --> 01:10:35,408
- Small is the answer. - Small is the answer.

2034
01:10:35,668 --> 01:10:38,408
- It's the answer. We're the answer! - We're the answer.

2035
01:10:39,338 --> 01:10:40,678
(Back in the day, what did we do whenever we came here?)

2036
01:10:41,078 --> 01:10:42,578
(We came to make memories in Boryeong!)

2037
01:10:42,578 --> 01:10:44,278
(Pretending to jump)

2038
01:10:44,518 --> 01:10:47,048
(Sixth Sense in Boryeong)

2039
01:10:47,448 --> 01:10:49,948
(A city packed with sights and endless fun)

2040
01:10:50,018 --> 01:10:51,018
- Hello, Jae Seok. - Hyun Ji.

2041
01:10:51,018 --> 01:10:52,888
(Shin Hyun Ji and Lee Sang Yeob)

2042
01:10:53,828 --> 01:10:55,228
(Why are your shoulders so puffed up?)

2043
01:10:55,688 --> 01:10:58,158
(I thought you were Park Hyun Bin from afar.)

2044
01:10:58,258 --> 01:10:59,258
(Laughing)

2045
01:10:59,258 --> 01:11:01,598
(The explosive chemistry of the original Sixth Sense crew)

2046
01:11:01,868 --> 01:11:04,298
(I noticed something slightly off.)

2047
01:11:04,368 --> 01:11:05,798
(There's no menu.)

2048
01:11:05,938 --> 01:11:08,068
(Coming up with deductions like that...)

2049
01:11:08,138 --> 01:11:09,338
(proves you're a total amateur.)

2050
01:11:09,338 --> 01:11:10,538
(Sulky)

2051
01:11:10,738 --> 01:11:13,178
(You're dull as ever.)

2052
01:11:13,508 --> 01:11:15,248
(You should only set expectations for someone who can deliver.)

2053
01:11:15,408 --> 01:11:19,118
(I just arrived from London earlier today.)

2054
01:11:19,278 --> 01:11:20,988
(I'm going to Barcelona the day after tomorrow.)

2055
01:11:21,148 --> 01:11:23,218
(A truly world-class top model)

2056
01:11:23,518 --> 01:11:25,358
(People used to ask me what I'd grow up to be,)

2057
01:11:25,358 --> 01:11:26,988
